1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,600
Welcome friends. In this video we'll be running through a quick overview of setting up Dastardly

2
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:15,000
with Jenkins. Dastardly is a free lightweight web application security scanner for your CICD

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,520
pipelines. It is designed specifically for web developers and checks your application for seven

4
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:25,800
security issues that are likely to interest you during software development. Dastardly is

5
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:32,880
based on the same scanner as Burp Suite. You can integrate Dastardly with any CICD platform but

6
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:38,640
in this video we'll be configuring Dastardly to run in Jenkins. For Dastardly system requirements we

7
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:44,340
recommend a minimum of four CPU cores and four gigabytes of RAM. In this video we are testing

8
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:51,800
Dastardly with Jenkins build 2.375. Additionally your Jenkins server or build node must have docker

9
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:58,240
installed but no plugins beyond the Jenkins defaults are required to run Dastardly in a Jenkins CICD

10
00:00:58,240 --> 00:01:05,660
pipeline. So let's begin. First within Jenkins we'll select a new item. We'll give this a name

11
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:12,740
Dastardly test. We'll select pipeline and click OK. Further we can give it a description here. We'll

12
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:18,420
jump right in and configure our pipeline. You can see we have two options. We can cut and paste the

13
00:01:18,420 --> 00:01:24,180
script directly into Jenkins or we can pull that from an SCM which is what we're going to do. Just

14
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:31,560
a quick note on the script itself. This is available on our website documentation for Dastardly and it's

15
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:38,040
going to pull the latest image of Dastardly for you and the output for the Dastardly report will be an

16
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:45,360
XML format and here is where we can also specify the Dastardly target URL. This is the URL of the

17
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:50,120
application that we want to scan. For the purpose of this video we're going to be scanning Jinn and

18
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:57,120
Juice which is our deliberately vulnerable web application. Now I can continue configuring my

19
00:01:57,120 --> 00:02:08,460
pipeline. I'm going to select git, add my credentials and the repository URL. Next I need to specify the

20
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:16,220
branch and for me that's going to be main and then my script path which is Jenkins file. Now I can save

21
00:02:16,220 --> 00:02:26,220
and build my pipeline. If I select the pipeline I can monitor the progress of the scan in the console output.

22
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:34,220
We can see Dastardly has started and has begun its scan of our target application. Scans will run for no more than 10 minutes.

23
00:02:34,220 --> 00:02:36,760
We'll fast forward here and we can check out the results.

24
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:50,880
Okay our scan is complete. We can see that the build has failed because we have located vulnerabilities within our application.

25
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:54,220
If we scroll back up to the top and select our build

26
00:02:54,220 --> 00:02:58,460
we can then view our test results.

27
00:02:58,460 --> 00:03:09,000
This will detail all failed tests for our particular build and we can select one to view the stack trace and this will give us detail on our particular vulnerability.

28
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,540
So we can see here we have a cross-site scripting found at this location.

29
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:19,540
We scroll into the stack trace. This is going to give us issue background information on that particular vulnerability.

30
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:25,540
Some remediation advice. We'll be able to view our evidence which includes the request and the response.

31
00:03:25,540 --> 00:03:34,000
There will also be a reference to our web security academy. This will include additional detail on the particular vulnerability as well as a sandbox environment to carry out the same kind of attack.

32
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:41,000
And finally our vulnerability classifications.

33
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,540
And that's it folks. As simple as that.

34
00:03:43,540 --> 00:03:51,540
You can check out our other dastardly docs to view configuration steps for running dastardly in TeamCity, GitHub Actions and our platform independent instructions.

35
00:03:51,540 --> 00:03:53,540
Thank you and take care.

